C++ doesn&#x27;t have methods&#x2F; &quot;member functions&quot; on the primitive types.<p>One reason is that C++ doesn&#x27;t know where to put them, for user defined class, struct or union types these &quot;member functions&quot; as their name suggests live inside the class&#x2F; struct&#x2F; union definition but for a primitive where would these live?<p>In Rust they&#x27;re just impl blocks, for example the method we&#x27;re discussing here is:<p><pre><code>    impl bool {    
        pub const fn toggle(&amp;mut self) {
            *self = !*self;
        }
    }</code></pre>

You can of course also do<p><pre><code>  long_path_bool ^= true;
</code></pre>
but that’s less obvious.

This gives me the same feeling as when I saw Rust&#x27;s map Entry API<p><a href="https:&#x2F;&#x2F;doc.rust-lang.org&#x2F;std&#x2F;collections&#x2F;hash_map&#x2F;enum.Entry.html#method.or_insert" rel="nofollow">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;doc.rust-lang.org&#x2F;std&#x2F;collections&#x2F;hash_map&#x2F;enum.Entr...</a><p><pre><code>    let mut map: HashMap&lt;&amp;str, u32&gt; = HashMap::new();
    map.entry(&quot;poneyland&quot;).or_insert(3);
    assert_eq!(map[&quot;poneyland&quot;], 3);
</code></pre>
It&#x27;s very ergonomic.

If you&#x27;re dealing with something like an `Option&lt;bool&gt;` or `Vec&lt;bool&gt;`, it might be nice to avoid needing to do a more verbose dereference and assign:<p><pre><code>    let mut vec = vec![false, false, false, false];
    vec[2].toggle();
    *vec[2] = !vec[2];
</code></pre>
&quot;Good enough&quot; is subjective, but I personally prefer the new way
